21:44:45 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: Yes—periods of distractibility and “being elsewhere” are common in school-age children of any gender. They may be preoccupied by friendships, worries, excitement, games, sleep changes, schoolwork, or simply normal developmental changes in attention and self-regulation. It’s worth paying closer attention if it is persistent and happens in several settings (home and school), is clearly affecting learning, relationships, or safety, or comes with signs such as major anxiety, low mood, sleep problems, hyperactivity/impulsivity, or frequent daydreaming that he cannot interrupt. Helpful first steps include: - Keep routines predictable, especially sleep and homework time. - Break tasks into short, clear steps with brief breaks. - Reduce distractions during work. - Ask him gently what has been on his mind rather than assuming he is not trying. - Check with his teacher about whether they notice the same pattern. If the problem has lasted for months or is causing significant difficulties, his parent or guardian can discuss it with a pediatrician or school support professional.
